1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:04,302
On a kitchen counter sits a

2
00:00:04,302 --> 00:00:06,956
four-hundred-dollar machine
backed by some of Silicon

3
00:00:06,996 --> 00:00:09,086
Valley's most prominent investors.

4
00:00:09,843 --> 00:00:12,730
Beside it, a sealed packet
of fruit and vegetables.

5
00:00:13,447 --> 00:00:18,881
In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters
conducted a remarkably simple experiment.

6
00:00:19,458 --> 00:00:23,348
Instead of using the machine, they
squeezed the packet with their hands.

7
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:24,990
Juice came out.

8
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:30,467
A company that had raised approximately
one hundred and twenty million dollars

9
00:00:30,507 --> 00:00:32,697
suddenly faced a devastating question.

10
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,604
What exactly were customers paying for?

11
00:00:37,299 --> 00:00:41,716
Juicero wasn't an obscure invention
struggling to attract attention.

12
00:00:42,296 --> 00:00:47,279
Its investors included Google Ventures,
Kleiner Perkins, and Campbell Soup.

13
00:00:47,797 --> 00:00:52,364
These were organizations with experience
in technology, consumer products, and

14
00:00:52,404 --> 00:00:53,874
ambitious new businesses.

15
00:00:54,537 --> 00:00:58,386
The opportunity appeared to
combine several attractive ideas.

16
00:00:58,886 --> 00:01:03,529
Healthy living, connected appliances,
convenience, and recurring purchases.

17
00:01:04,022 --> 00:01:07,530
Juicero wasn't merely
selling a kitchen appliance.

18
00:01:08,365 --> 00:01:13,575
It was attempting to build an entire
commercial ecosystem around the daily habit of

19
00:01:13,615 --> 00:01:14,925
drinking fresh juice.

20
00:01:16,735 --> 00:01:21,563
Behind the company was Doug Evans, an
entrepreneur who believed fresh produce should

21
00:01:21,603 --> 00:01:24,173
become a more convenient
part of people's lives.

22
00:01:24,652 --> 00:01:28,664
Traditional juicing required
preparation, equipment, and cleanup.

23
00:01:29,244 --> 00:01:33,657
Buying prepared juice was easier, but
Juicero offered another possibility.

24
00:01:34,363 --> 00:01:39,082
Fresh juice at home without handling loose
ingredients or cleaning a conventional

25
00:01:39,122 --> 00:01:40,122
juicer.

26
00:01:40,515 --> 00:01:43,382
The underlying consumer
problem was understandable.

27
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:48,219
The controversial decision was how Evans
and his company chose to solve it.

28
00:01:48,578 --> 00:01:52,621
They approached a food preparation
problem as a technology platform.

29
00:01:54,636 --> 00:02:00,379
In March 2016, Juicero launched
its connected countertop press.

30
00:02:01,001 --> 00:02:03,372
The price was six hundred
and ninety-nine dollars.

31
00:02:04,488 --> 00:02:09,076
Customers inserted proprietary packets
containing prepared fruits and vegetables.

32
00:02:09,606 --> 00:02:14,186
The machine pressed their contents into a
glass, without requiring the customer to

33
00:02:14,226 --> 00:02:15,616
handle the loose produce.

34
00:02:16,175 --> 00:02:19,218
It was designed to make juicing
cleaner and more consistent.

35
00:02:19,877 --> 00:02:22,630
But the starting price created
an immediate challenge.

36
00:02:23,273 --> 00:02:27,919
Before Juicero could sell millions of
servings, it first had to convince customers

37
00:02:27,959 --> 00:02:31,549
to purchase an unusually
expensive appliance.

38
00:02:33,515 --> 00:02:35,878
Selling the machine was
only the beginning.

39
00:02:36,409 --> 00:02:41,308
Juicero also supplied the refrigerated
produce packets used by the press.

40
00:02:41,917 --> 00:02:45,006
Each purchase created another
opportunity for revenue.

41
00:02:45,552 --> 00:02:50,889
The model resembled other appliance businesses
built around compatible consumables.

42
00:02:51,429 --> 00:02:56,240
But fresh vegetables are different from
coffee capsules or printer cartridges.

43
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:57,686
They deteriorate.

44
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:01,312
They require careful handling
and dependable distribution.

45
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:06,599
Juicero's business therefore depended
on two products succeeding together.

46
00:03:07,082 --> 00:03:11,953
Customers needed a reason to buy the machine,
and then a reason to keep buying the

47
00:03:11,993 --> 00:03:12,993
packets.

48
00:03:14,431 --> 00:03:20,294
Juicero's press incorporated internet
connectivity and a system for identifying its

49
00:03:20,334 --> 00:03:21,334
produce packets.

50
00:03:21,870 --> 00:03:26,034
The company argued that this technology
helped protect freshness and improve

51
00:03:26,074 --> 00:03:27,074
consistency.

52
00:03:27,651 --> 00:03:31,625
It was trying to control the experience
from delivery to consumption.

53
00:03:32,276 --> 00:03:35,626
But every additional feature
introduced another question.

54
00:03:36,196 --> 00:03:41,178
Did customers value the technology enough
to justify the price and dependence on a

55
00:03:41,218 --> 00:03:42,488
proprietary system?

56
00:03:42,952 --> 00:03:46,535
Juicero had created a
tightly integrated product.

57
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:51,605
That integration could offer benefits, but
it also made the appliance dependent on

58
00:03:51,645 --> 00:03:52,695
the wider business.

59
00:03:54,530 --> 00:03:58,943
The Juicero Press was not simply an
empty shell with a motor inside.

60
00:03:59,599 --> 00:04:04,249
Its construction involved a sophisticated
mechanical system and specialized

61
00:04:04,289 --> 00:04:05,289
components.

62
00:04:06,018 --> 00:04:09,735
A third-party teardown revealed
the complexity of the engineering.

63
00:04:10,325 --> 00:04:14,157
From that perspective, the machine
was an ambitious piece of hardware.

64
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:19,114
But engineering achievement and
commercial value are not the same thing.

65
00:04:19,594 --> 00:04:23,469
A product can be technically impressive
while remaining difficult to justify

66
00:04:23,509 --> 00:04:24,509
economically.

67
00:04:25,176 --> 00:04:29,418
The more complicated the solution becomes,
the more important it is to establish

68
00:04:29,458 --> 00:04:32,308
that customers genuinely
need what it delivers.

69
00:04:34,255 --> 00:04:39,044
Before the infamous experiment, Juicero
had already changed its pricing strategy.

70
00:04:39,503 --> 00:04:43,168
In January 2017, the company reduced the
price of its press from six hundred and

71
00:04:43,208 --> 00:04:46,758
ninety-nine dollars to three
hundred and ninety-nine.

72
00:04:47,584 --> 00:04:50,450
That's a reduction of
roughly forty-three percent.

73
00:04:51,027 --> 00:04:55,350
Lowering the price made the product
accessible to more potential customers.

74
00:04:55,873 --> 00:05:01,572
But it also demonstrated that the original
price was difficult to sustain as a route

75
00:05:01,612 --> 00:05:02,213
to wider adoption.

76
00:05:02,253 --> 00:05:06,296
And the company had not yet faced the
public revelation that would transform its

77
00:05:06,336 --> 00:05:07,336
reputation.

78
00:05:08,858 --> 00:05:10,718
Then came the Bloomberg investigation.

79
00:05:11,438 --> 00:05:16,016
Reporters compared the output of Juicero's
machine with what they could extract

80
00:05:16,056 --> 00:05:18,986
manually from the same type of packet.

81
00:05:19,483 --> 00:05:23,608
The machine produced slightly more juice,
but hand squeezing delivered a remarkably

82
00:05:23,648 --> 00:05:25,098
similar result.

83
00:05:25,628 --> 00:05:29,628
In the documented comparison, the
manual method was also faster.

84
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:34,166
The difference wasn't enough to make
every benefit of the appliance disappear.

85
00:05:34,867 --> 00:05:37,402
But it exposed a much larger problem.

86
00:05:37,925 --> 00:05:39,368
The most visible function of

87
00:05:39,368 --> 00:05:42,047
a four-hundred-dollar machine
could be substantially

88
00:05:42,087 --> 00:05:44,337
reproduced without buying it.

89
00:05:45,974 --> 00:05:50,662
Until then, Juicero's story had been
about technology improving nutrition and

90
00:05:50,702 --> 00:05:52,012
convenience.

91
00:05:52,711 --> 00:05:55,884
The investigation introduced
a much simpler narrative.

92
00:05:56,451 --> 00:06:00,568
An expensive machine performing a task
people could accomplish themselves.

93
00:06:01,350 --> 00:06:05,286
That contrast was easy to understand
and difficult to overcome.

94
00:06:05,930 --> 00:06:10,699
The brand's challenge was no longer just
explaining why its product was useful.

95
00:06:11,201 --> 00:06:14,801
It had to defend why the complete
system was worth purchasing.

96
00:06:15,401 --> 00:06:19,903
And it had to do so while its hardware
became the subject of widespread public

97
00:06:19,943 --> 00:06:20,943
criticism.

98
00:06:22,538 --> 00:06:27,920
Juicero's leadership rejected the idea
that its value could be measured solely by

99
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:30,030
hand squeezing a packet.

100
00:06:30,595 --> 00:06:35,438
Chief executive Jeff Dunn defended the
broader system, emphasizing convenience,

101
00:06:35,628 --> 00:06:38,038
product consistency, and quality control.

102
00:06:38,601 --> 00:06:41,972
The company also offered
dissatisfied customers refunds.

103
00:06:42,739 --> 00:06:44,945
Those arguments deserve consideration.

104
00:06:45,699 --> 00:06:50,300
A machine can provide benefits beyond
its most basic mechanical function.

105
00:06:50,982 --> 00:06:55,643
But Juicero now needed to demonstrate that
those additional benefits justified the

106
00:06:55,683 --> 00:06:56,753
cost.

107
00:06:57,302 --> 00:07:02,129
Its defense depended on customers valuing
the complete experience rather than simply

108
00:07:02,169 --> 00:07:03,169
the juice.

109
00:07:04,715 --> 00:07:09,215
The hand-squeezing experiment became the
defining image of Juicero's failure.

110
00:07:09,789 --> 00:07:12,018
But behind that image
was another challenge.

111
00:07:12,667 --> 00:07:17,378
Every produce packet had to be sourced,
prepared, packaged, transported, and

112
00:07:17,418 --> 00:07:19,468
delivered while maintaining freshness.

113
00:07:19,978 --> 00:07:23,669
The business was operating two
demanding systems at once.

114
00:07:24,235 --> 00:07:26,889
Consumer hardware and
fresh-food distribution.

115
00:07:27,601 --> 00:07:30,506
Increasing sales meant
expanding that infrastructure.

116
00:07:31,210 --> 00:07:36,613
And reducing prices meant finding ways to
deliver the same experience at lower cost.

117
00:07:36,982 --> 00:07:38,642
The appliance attracted attention.

118
00:07:39,262 --> 00:07:44,059
The distribution network represented a less
visible, but equally important, business

119
00:07:44,099 --> 00:07:45,099
problem.

120
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:52,027
By July 2017, Juicero was pursuing
another attempt to make its system more

121
00:07:52,067 --> 00:07:53,067
affordable.

122
00:07:53,530 --> 00:07:57,783
The company announced plans to reduce
staffing by twenty-five percent and work

123
00:07:57,823 --> 00:08:00,873
toward lower prices for its
appliance and produce packs.

124
00:08:01,464 --> 00:08:06,224
Contemporary reporting described ambitions
for a press costing less than two hundred

125
00:08:06,264 --> 00:08:07,264
dollars.

126
00:08:07,945 --> 00:08:11,081
These were plans, not a
successful commercial turnaround.

127
00:08:11,789 --> 00:08:16,065
Juicero was attempting to restructure its
costs while defending a damaged product

128
00:08:16,105 --> 00:08:17,375
proposition.

129
00:08:17,986 --> 00:08:22,204
It needed cheaper hardware, cheaper
distribution, and enough customers to support

130
00:08:22,244 --> 00:08:23,244
both.

131
00:08:24,681 --> 00:08:30,353
On September first, 2017, Juicero announced
that it was suspending sales of its

132
00:08:30,393 --> 00:08:32,123
press and produce packets.

133
00:08:32,753 --> 00:08:37,232
The company said it had sold more than a
million produce packs since launching.

134
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:42,978
But it concluded that the infrastructure
necessary to serve a national customer base

135
00:08:43,018 --> 00:08:46,168
was beyond what it could
establish independently.

136
00:08:46,815 --> 00:08:51,630
Juicero began seeking an acquirer with
an existing fresh-food supply chain and

137
00:08:51,670 --> 00:08:53,760
offered refunds on its presses.

138
00:08:54,472 --> 00:08:59,201
Approximately seventeen months after
launch, the ambitious consumer technology

139
00:08:59,241 --> 00:09:02,071
business could no longer
continue independently.

140
00:09:04,009 --> 00:09:06,780
The evidence points toward
three strategic weaknesses.

141
00:09:07,482 --> 00:09:11,922
An expensive appliance placed a high
barrier in front of customer adoption.

142
00:09:12,522 --> 00:09:18,101
A proprietary fresh-food ecosystem
introduced significant operational demands.

143
00:09:18,770 --> 00:09:22,481
And the product's central function could
be reproduced through a dramatically

144
00:09:22,521 --> 00:09:23,911
cheaper alternative.

145
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:29,371
None of these observations alone
explains every decision inside Juicero.

146
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,869
Together, however, they reveal a business
struggling to align engineering, customer

147
00:09:34,909 --> 00:09:37,419
value, and distribution economics.

148
00:09:38,123 --> 00:09:41,591
Venture funding created the
opportunity to build the system.

149
00:09:42,072 --> 00:09:45,302
It couldn't guarantee that the
system would become sustainable.

150
00:09:47,125 --> 00:09:51,366
Juicero wasn't a failure because
innovation is unnecessary.

151
00:09:52,016 --> 00:09:57,066
It was a reminder that innovation
must justify its own complexity.

152
00:09:57,106 --> 00:10:01,571
Approximately one hundred and twenty
million dollars in funding helped create an

153
00:10:01,611 --> 00:10:02,981
extraordinary machine.

154
00:10:03,521 --> 00:10:08,204
But customers still needed a compelling
reason to buy it, and the company needed an

155
00:10:08,244 --> 00:10:10,094
affordable way to support it.

156
00:10:10,531 --> 00:10:15,744
The most important lesson wasn't revealed
inside a boardroom or a financial report.

157
00:10:16,333 --> 00:10:19,517
It was revealed when someone
picked up a packet and squeezed.

158
00:10:20,237 --> 00:10:22,041
Every brand leaves evidence.
